Subject: [PATCH 0/4] drm/msm/dsi: add the bonded command path for SM8750

*** AI-assistance disclosure ***: this series was drafted by a coding
agent (Pi) working from the author's design and measurements; the author
reviewed, built and approved the final text. The design, the measurements
and the decisions are the author's own.

Upstream drives a bonded DSI pair for clocks and PHY only. dsi.xml has
described the broadcast bit of CMD_DMA_CTRL all along and dsi_host.c has
never set it (git show 6628e7d4c6:drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c |
grep -c BROADCAST -> 0), so no code path can send one command over both
links.

On this board (SM8750, 1904x3040, bonded C-PHY + DSC) that is directly
observable. The panel's first DCS command goes out on one link only and
fails:

    panel-novatek-nt36536 ae94000.dsi.0: sending dcs data ff 20 failed: -22
    (three attempts, then "Failed to initialize panel: -22";
     reports/b5-step4-master-from-cfg-20261027.md)

with the command DMA engine left armed and never completing
(STATUS0=0x20003, TRIG_DMA=0x1 self-clearing never happens;
patches/upstream/m6-b5-p4-rfc-20261027/RFC-SCOPE.md). Staging the peer
first and arming the master last, with the completion IRQ on the master
alone, removes all of it: the seven error counters stay at zero in ten
consecutive blank/wake cycles on the same hardware.

So the missing piece is a command transfer over the broadcast path. This
series adds exactly that, and nothing else:

  1/4 describes the registers the path needs and puts the per-SoC values
      it uses (staged command-DMA bits, FIFO error bits, poll budget)
      into the existing per-SoC config table. SM8750 reports the same 6G
      v2.9 revision as SM8650, so its entry is matched on the compatible;
      every other SoC leaves the pointer NULL.
  2/4 implements the transaction as a host op: both engines are staged
      with the same packet, the peer is started first and polled, the
      master is armed last and owns the completion interrupt. Whatever the
      transaction reprograms is saved and restored.
  3/4 routes bonded writes from the manager through that op. The master
      link is the one the DT marks with "qcom,master-dsi" -
      dsi_mgr_parse_of() stores it in master_dsi_link_id and the link ids
      are the DSI host indices, not the probe order. The DT stays the
      authority for the role.
  4/4 rejects a bonded command issued on the peer link with a message that
      names both links, instead of arming the engines on a link that does
      not drive them.

3/4 is also where the per-SoC data becomes the switch. The host op lives in
the 6G v2.9 op set, which SM8650 shares, so "the op exists" cannot mean
"this SoC can run the transaction": SM8650 has no bonded data and every
SM8650 dual-DSI pair in the tree (sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2, for one) would
otherwise take a path built from an empty config. The manager therefore
requires the per-SoC data as well, and an SoC without it keeps the old
path. The alternative - a second copy of the v2.9 op table with the field
left out - would duplicate the whole table to express one bit of
information.

There is no separate "move the state" patch: the failure flag and the
mutex are introduced where they belong from the start, in msm_dsim_glb
next to is_bonded_dsi/is_sync_needed, so no mutable bonded state is
file-scoped at any point in the series.

On who needs this: no board in the tree enables a command-mode bonded
pair today, so the path is exercised by the SM8750 board this work started
from, whose panel driver and DT are being prepared as a separate series.
The existing dual-DSI boards keep the behaviour they have now, which the
gate in 3/4 is what guarantees.

Verified on top of v7.4-rc1 (6628e7d4c6):
  - checkpatch --strict: 0 errors, 0 warnings on all four patches (one
    CHECK on the 10 us poll delay, kept: the peer poll is a tight
    register loop, bounded to 5 ms and run once per command);
  - arm64 make -j26 W=1 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/: rc=0, no warnings;
  - the four patches apply in order to 6628e7d4c6 with git am.

Qianhui Xu (4):
  drm/msm/dsi: add per-SoC bonded command-path config
  drm/msm/dsi: add the bonded command broadcast host ops
  drm/msm/dsi: route bonded commands through the DSI manager
  drm/msm/dsi: reject bonded commands issued on the peer link

